Workflow: Policies

Purpose:
  Define the governance, authorization, and policy engine for the
  SDDRA system. Policies are deterministic rules that govern what
  agents can do, under which conditions, with which tools, and when
  human approval is required.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Governance model
  Capability vs Permission vs Policy distinction
  Rule, Constraint, Guard, Gate, Approval definitions
  Policy Engine architecture and evaluation
  Authorization request and decision flow
  Decision types and precedence
  Policy scopes (Global, Project, Domain, Task, Agent, Skill, Environment, Resource)
  Policy composition and deny-by-default
  Immutable policies and policy versioning
  Policy snapshots and decision logs
  Pre-execution and post-execution policy
  Tool sandbox and permission matrix
  Human-in-the-loop and approval lifecycle
  Conditional policies and policy chains

Principles:
  Capability != Permission != Policy.
  Agent declares what it can do; Policy Engine decides what is allowed.
  Deny by default — explicitly allowed actions only.
  Policy is deterministic; AI does not override policy.
  Critical denies cannot be overridden by lower-level allows.
  History is immutable — policy decisions are audited.

Governance:
  definition:
    system that defines what AI agents can do, under which conditions,
    and when human approval is required

  model:
    Capability -> Permission -> Policy -> Guard -> Gate -> Approval -> Tool -> Execution -> Validation

  rule: governance is enforced by deterministic engine, not AI

Capability:
  definition:
    concrete ability to perform specific work

  examples:
    - GO
    - POSTGRESQL
    - REST
    - KUBERNETES
    - SECURITY-REVIEW

  registry:
    CAP-GO
    CAP-POSTGRES
    CAP-KAFKA
    CAP-KUBERNETES
    CAP-SECURITY

  rule: capability = can do (technical ability)

Permission:
  definition:
    authorization to perform an action on a resource

  examples:
    CAN read source
    CANNOT write production

  rule: permission = may do (authorization)
  rule: capability does not imply permission

Policy:
  definition:
    rule that determines under which conditions an action is allowed

  examples:
    Production database migration -> only DevOps agent -> requires approval -> backup must exist

  rule: policy = under what conditions is action allowed

Rule:
  definition:
    concrete conditional statement

  example:
    IF environment == production
    THEN approval_required = true

Constraint:
  definition:
    boundary that limits an action

  example:
    write_scope: services/auth/**

  rule: agent cannot modify resources outside constraint

Guard:
  definition:
    pre-execution check that evaluates policy before action

  flow:
    Action -> Guard -> ALLOW / DENY

  example:
    DROP TABLE -> Production Guard -> DENY

  rule: guard evaluation is deterministic

Gate:
  definition:
    workflow checkpoint that must be passed before stage advancement

  example:
    Implementation -> Tests -> Security Gate -> Deployment

  rule: gate MUST be passed before workflow advances

Approval:
  definition:
    explicit authorization from human or trusted actor

  flow:
    AI -> REQUEST APPROVAL -> Human -> APPROVED -> EXECUTE

  rule: approval is specific to action/resource, not general
  rule: approval MUST have expiration

PolicyEngine:
  responsibilities:
    - resolve applicable policies
    - evaluate rules
    - check constraints
    - check permissions
    - check approval requirements
    - return decision

  flow:
    Request -> Resolve Policies -> Evaluate Rules -> Check Constraints -> Check Permissions -> Check Approval -> ALLOW / DENY / REQUIRE_APPROVAL

AuthorizationRequest:
  structure:
    agent: AGENT-CODER-01
    task: TASK-AUTH-001
    action: WRITE
    resource: auth/login.go
    environment: development

PolicyDecision:
  structure:
    result: ALLOW | DENY | REQUIRE_APPROVAL | ALLOW_WITH_CONSTRAINTS
    policy: DEV-WRITE-001
    reason: resource_in_write_scope
    constraints: max_files=5

DecisionTypes:
  ALLOW:
    description: action is permitted

  DENY:
    description: action is not permitted
    rule: deny overrides any lower-level allow

  REQUIRE_APPROVAL:
    description: action requires explicit human approval before execution

  ALLOW_WITH_CONSTRAINTS:
    description: action is permitted with additional constraints
    example: ALLOW but max_files=5

PolicyEvaluation:
  algorithm:
    policies = resolve(request)
    for policy in policies:
      result = evaluate(policy, request)
      if result == DENY:
        return DENY
      if result == REQUIRE_APPROVAL:
        return REQUIRE_APPROVAL
    return ALLOW

  rule: first DENY wins
  rule: deny cannot be overridden by lower-level allow

PolicyPrecedence:
  hierarchy:
    GLOBAL -> PROJECT -> DOMAIN -> TASK -> AGENT -> SKILL -> ENVIRONMENT -> RESOURCE

  rule: higher level overrides lower level
  rule: critical deny at any level cannot be overridden

DenyByDefault:
  rule: explicitly allowed actions only
  rule: unknown actions default to DENY
  rule: this is especially critical for AI systems

PolicyScope:
  GLOBAL:
    applies_to: entire system
    example: secrets cannot be exposed
    override: cannot be overridden by any lower level

  PROJECT:
    applies_to: entire project
    example: all code must pass tests

  DOMAIN:
    applies_to: specific domain
    example: PAYMENT: financial operations require audit

  TASK:
    applies_to: specific task
    example: TASK-PAYMENT-REFUND: refund requires approval

  AGENT:
    applies_to: specific agent
    example: AGENT-QA: read-only source access

  SKILL:
    applies_to: specific skill
    example: SKILL-DEPLOY: production deployment requires approval

  ENVIRONMENT:
    applies_to: specific environment
    example:
      development: autonomous
      staging: autonomous + tests
      production: approval required

  RESOURCE:
    applies_to: specific resource
    example:
      resource: production/database
      policy: DELETE = DENY

PolicyComposition:
  flow:
    Global -> Project -> Domain -> Task -> Agent -> Skill -> Environment -> Resource

  rule: all policies contribute to final decision
  rule: deny at any level cannot be overridden

ImmutablePolicies:
  examples:
    - GLOBAL_SECURITY_POLICY
    - DATA_PROTECTION_POLICY

  rule: AI cannot modify immutable policies
  rule: not even ARCHITECT agent can override immutable policies

PolicyVersioning:
  rule: policies are versioned
  example:
    SECURITY-001 v1
    SECURITY-001 v2

  rule: execution records which policy version was applied

PolicySnapshot:
  rule: execution creates policy snapshot
  example:
    EXEC-100 -> POLICY-SNAPSHOT-100

  rule: snapshot records all active policies at execution time
  rule: snapshot enables answering "Why was this action denied?"

PolicyDecisionLog:
  structure:
    decision:
      id: DEC-100
      agent: AGENT-001
      task: TASK-100
      action: WRITE
      resource: auth/login.go
      result: ALLOW
      policy: DEV-WRITE-01
      timestamp: ...

  rule: every critical authorization MUST be logged
  rule: decision log is append-only

PreExecutionPolicy:
  flow:
    Agent -> Tool Request -> Policy Engine -> ALLOW -> Tool

  rule: policy is evaluated BEFORE tool execution
  rule: this is the primary security guard

PostExecutionPolicy:
  flow:
    Tool -> Output -> Policy Validator -> ACCEPT / REJECT

  example:
    Agent adds source code -> Tool succeeds -> Post-policy detects SECRET -> REJECT

  rule: output validation can reject successful tool execution

ToolSandbox:
  definition:
    restricted environment where tools operate

  example:
    ALLOWED: read, write, git, test
    BLOCKED: network, production-db

  rule: tool sandbox enforces permission boundaries

ToolPermissionMatrix:
  | Tool         | Dev | Staging | Prod |
  | ------------ | --: | ------: | ---: |
  | Read         |   ✅ |       ✅ |    ✅ |
  | Write        |   ✅ |       ✅ |   ⚠️ |
  | Test         |   ✅ |       ✅ |   ⚠️ |
  | Git push     |   ✅ |      ⚠️ |    ❌ |
  | Deploy       |   ❌ |      ⚠️ |   ⚠️ |
  | DB migration |   ✅ |      ⚠️ |   🔒 |

HumanInTheLoop:
  flow:
    AI -> Policy Engine -> REQUIRE_APPROVAL -> Human -> Approve -> Tool

  rule: critical actions REQUIRE human approval
  rule: production, financial, security, data deletion require approval

ApprovalScope:
  rule: approval MUST be specific to action and resource
  example:
    APPROVE: AGENT-001, TASK-100, WRITE, production/config.yaml

  rule: approval does not grant blanket permissions

ApprovalExpiration:
  rule: approval MUST have expiration
  example: approved for next 30 minutes

  rule: expired approvals require re-approval

ApprovalReplayProtection:
  rule: approval is bound to specific request
  rule: approval cannot be reused for different actions

ConditionalPolicy:
  example:
    IF tests_passed
    AND security_scan_passed
    AND environment == staging
    THEN ALLOW

  rule: policies can have multiple conditions

PolicyChain:
  definition:
    sequence of policy checks required for workflow advancement

  example:
    TASK -> BUILD -> UNIT TEST -> INTEGRATION TEST -> SECURITY SCAN -> APPROVAL -> DEPLOY

  rule: all gates in chain MUST pass

PolicyVsValidation:
  Policy:
    definition: is this action allowed?
    example: Can agent modify auth/login.go?

  Validation:
    definition: is the result correct?
    example: Does code compile?

  rule: policy and validation are separate layers

Navigation:
  Workflow: @../workflow/INDEX.sdd
  Gates: @../gates/INDEX.sdd
  Runtime: @../runtime/INDEX.sdd
  Orchestrator: @../orchestrator/INDEX.sdd
  Protocol: @../protocol/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [WP-01] Capability does not imply permission — policy engine enforces authorization.
  [WP-02] Deny by default — explicitly allowed actions only.
  [WP-03] Critical deny cannot be overridden by lower-level allow.
  [WP-04] Policy evaluation is deterministic — AI does not override policy.
  [WP-05] Immutable policies cannot be modified by any agent.
  [WP-06] Policy decisions are logged for audit trail.
  [WP-07] Pre-execution policy is the primary security guard.
  [WP-08] Post-execution policy can reject successful tool output.
  [WP-09] Tool sandbox enforces permission boundaries.
  [WP-10] Human approval is REQUIRED for critical actions.
  [WP-11] Approval is specific to action/resource and has expiration.
  [WP-12] Policy version is recorded with execution for reproducibility.
  [WP-13] Conditional policies MUST have explicit conditions.
  [WP-14] Policy chain MUST be passed before workflow advancement.
  [WP-15] Policy and validation are separate layers.
  [WP-16] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to any output.
  [WP-17] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in headers, titles, or messages — reserved for human use only.

State: +
